Doug Freyburger wrote:

 > With hostile mines I practice a cycle.  Go down and fight some.  If
 > possible eat some.  Then run back up the stairs and wander until
 > fully healed.  Back down the stairs.  Keep cycling until the entire
 > level is cleared and explored.  Then cycle at the next level.
 > There's no need to count kills.

That's quite a sensible approach and it works most of the time, esp.
if you stay close to the upstairs. I also like to pick up weapons and
armor and stash them on the (cleared) level above so monsters cannot
use them against me anymore.

 > With peaceful mines I still want the objects of the peaceful
 > monsters so I count kills.  Four hostiles per peaceful.  If I fully
 > explore and clear a level of hostiles and it still has peacefuls I
 > tend or not based on health and other criteria.  Usually down to
 > the next level I go. With peaceful mines the density of hostiles is
 > lower so there is more chance to eat corpses and stay well fed. Eat
 > at the first sign of hunger.

Doesn't that approach tend to leave quite a few peacefuls behind on
each level? There simply aren't enough hostiles (that 4:1 ratio) in
my experience.

Leaving a potential mithril-coat behind can make all the difference.
That's why I generally want their items as soon as possible and the
"let your pet kill them" approach manages that nicely.

 > Having learned that difference in strategy it stopped being an
 > advantage or disadvantage.  Just a call for different strategies.

My only problem with hostile mines is wand-zapping monsters.

It's one of the few things the game can kill me with these days. (The
vast majority of deaths result from my own stupidity #-).) That's why
I see peaceful mines as major advantage.

